# fmt-writes.allow -- the bounded writes still to move onto the owner of a
# formatted text (we_fmt.h), counted per function (tests/check-fmt-writes.sh,
# rule R2).  A line is RULE|file|function|count|why.  Every call listed is
# memory safe (its size is its storage's own) and holds no value of the write
# and no offset: what is left to decide, site by site, is what a text that
# does not fit becomes (refused, or cut and marked).  The counts can only
# fall; a line whose count is above what the scan finds fails as stale.
# MARK lines: the one other owner's marker of a cut row (the message box's).
# The one format that is not a literal and is left as it is: we_fl_unix.c
# (WpeHandleFileManager) printf(e_msg[ERR_EXEC], filen) writes to stdout, a
# stream, which is out of this rule's class (the stream is the storage).
